<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Real-World Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct real-world experiments on a Unitree G1 humanoid robot equipped with a Dex3-1 dexterous hand for manipulation and a head-mounted RGB-D camera for egocentric perception.
Our system is deployed in a two-computer setup.
A desktop workstation with an RTX 5080 GPU runs the perception and decision modules, including SAM3-based multi-object segmentation, the VLM-based planning module, and the geometry-grounded supervisor.
The robot’s onboard Unitree computer runs the low-level controllers, including the arm control stack and the RL locomotion policy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments are performed in an indoor office environment that includes typical furniture and everyday objects.
The scene is cluttered to induce partial observability, occlusions, and viewpoint changes, providing a challenging testbed for loco-manipulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the agent on a suite of long-horizon humanoid loco-manipulation tasks.
To enable direct comparison with Being-0, we include the same tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>:
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Fetch-bottle</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Deliver-box</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Grasp-bottle</span>, <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Place-box</span>, and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Place-coffee</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond these, we introduce additional long-horizon tasks not reported in Being-0 to stress open-ended instruction following and multi-object tabletop reasoning:
(i) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tidy-desk</span>, which requires repeatedly picking and placing multiple objects until the desk is cleared.
(ii) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tabletop-sorting</span>, which requires categorizing objects into different containers.
(iii) <span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">I’m in a bad mood, bring me a drink</span>, which requires fetching a drink and handing it over to the user.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results on Being-0-aligned Multi-step Tasks</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report success rates on the Being-0-aligned multi-step task suite in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-B Results on Being-0-aligned Multi-step Tasks ‣ IV EXPERIMENTS ‣ Cybo-Waiter: A Physical Agentic Framework for Humanoid Whole-Body Locomotion–Manipulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.
We follow the task definitions and success criteria from Being-0 as closely as possible, and report our results over 10 trials per task.
Across multi-step routines, our agent achieves reliable completion by combining structured task programs with multi-object 3D grounding and predicate-based supervision.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Comparing the proposed method with being-0, the results show the number of successful manipulations in 10 trials.</figcaption>
